  "question": {
    "text": "A red solid is insoluble in water. However it becomes soluble if some $\\mathrm{KI}$ is added to water. Heating the red solid in a test tube results in liberation of some violet coloured fumes and droplets of a metal appear on the cooler parts of the test tube. The red solid is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{HgI}_2$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{HgO}$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\mathrm{Pb}_3 \\mathrm{O}_4$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_4\\right)_2 \\mathrm{Cr}_2 \\mathrm{O}_7$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\underset{\\text { (insoluble) }}{\\mathrm{HgI}_2}+\\mathrm{KI} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text { (soluble) }}{\\mathrm{K}_2 \\mathrm{HgI}_4}$\nOn heating $\\mathrm{HgI}_2$ decomposes as $\\mathrm{HgI}_2 \\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Hg}+\\mathrm{I}_2$"
  }
}